{
  "_sources": {
    "orbits.standish": "JPL, E.M. Standish, 'Approximate Positions of the Planets', Table 1 (1800–2050), mean ecliptic and equinox of J2000. Rates are per Julian century.",
    "orbits.keplerian": "JPL Solar System Dynamics, planetary satellite mean elements. Precession periods in Julian years, negative = regression.",
    "physical": "NASA Planetary Fact Sheets (nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet): volumetric mean radius, obliquity to orbit, sidereal rotation period (negative = retrograde). Informational; orientation comes from 'rotation'. Phobos: mean radius is the volumetric mean of the fact-sheet triaxial radii 13.0 × 11.4 × 9.1 km; rotation is synchronous (fact-sheet period 0.31891 d), obliquity to orbit taken as 0.",
    "render": "Texture paths are relative to the site base (public/). Sources and licences: public/textures/CREDITS.md.",
    "rotation": "IAU WGCCRE rotational elements (Archinal et al. 2011/2018): north pole RA/Dec in J2000 equatorial coordinates and prime meridian W = w0 + wDot·d, d = days since J2000. Pole and W follow IAU conventions exactly (Venus and Uranus: negative wDot; Pluto: positive pole). Moon and Neptune: periodic terms evaluated at J2000 and folded into the constants (the Moon's real pole circles with its 18.6-year node cycle). Mars: 2009 report values. Phobos: IAU 2015 values with the M1–M4 periodic terms evaluated at J2000."
  },
